(* Go+ v0.19.0 grammar delta: explicit impossible arms.

   The complete base grammar is Go plus the declarations and expressions
   in grammar-v0.18.0.ebnf. One production is added to match arms, in
   both surfaces (Stage C of the dependent-typing roadmap; gap program
   C1a, spec/gap-program-design.md).
*)

ImpossibleArm = "case" Pattern ":" "impossible" .        (* .gp *)
              (* goml: "|" Pattern "=>" "impossible" *)

(*
  ## Pruning becomes statable

  Since v0.7.0 an arm whose variant clashes with the scrutinee's indices
  is PRUNED by inference: `First(v Vec[T, n+1])` needs no Nil arm, and
  writing one was the error "pattern Nil() can never match". What could
  not be written was the author's own assertion of that fact. Idris2
  spells it `impossible`; now both surfaces do:

      match v {                        -- .gp
      case Cons(h, t): return h
      case Nil(): impossible
      }

      let First : Vec a (n + 1) -> a   -- goml
        | Cons h _ => h
        | Nil => impossible

  An impossible arm is CHECKED, then DROPPED: the checker requires the
  variant to be ruled out — by index clash under the hypotheses in
  scope, or by GADT result incompatibility — and the arm emits nothing.
  The generated Go is byte-identical to the omitted-arm form, boundary
  guards included.

  - A reachable arm marked impossible is an error naming what failed:
    "this arm is not impossible: the scrutinee's index (n+1) does not
    rule out Cons (its index is n+1)".
  - `case _: impossible` is an error: a wildcard matches anything the
    indices allow.
  - An impossible arm takes a single pattern and cannot bind — there is
    no value to name.

  ## The spelling costs no grammar

  In `.gp`, a bare `impossible` identifier statement is never valid Go
  (an undefined name, and an unused expression even when defined), so
  recognizing it in a case body preserves the strict-superset rule with
  no parser change. In goml, `impossible` joins the reserved words.
*)
